course: biology
lesson: 104
title: Koalas, wombats, Tasmanian devils and the platypus
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Most of the strange animals in this part of the zoo are marsupials, mammals whose tiny babies finish growing in a pouch, and two of them, the platypus and the echidna, are even stranger: mammals that lay eggs.
explain: The koala looks like a teddy bear that sleeps all day, and it really does rest up to about 20 hours, because its only food, eucalyptus leaves, has little energy and poisons that take hard work to digest.
explain: Go into the dark nocturnal room and your eyes slowly find a bilby with huge ears sniffing about and a sugar glider sailing from branch to branch on flaps of skin, while the platypus hunts in its tank with its eyes shut.
explain: Taz of Looney Tunes spins like a tornado and eats everything, and cartoons call the koala a bear; a real Tasmanian devil does not spin, though it screeches and eats bones and fur, and the koala is no bear at all.
fact: Koalas rest or sleep up to about 20 hours a day because eucalyptus leaves give little energy and contain poisons that take a lot of work to digest; koalas are marsupials, closer to wombats than to bears.
fact: Out of hundreds of kinds of eucalyptus each koala eats only a few, choosing less poisonous and more watery leaves, so zoos outside Australia grow eucalyptus plantations or fly in fresh leaves.
fact: On hot days koalas hug tree trunks, which are cooler than the air, to lose heat, and koalas have fingerprints very like a person's.
fact: Wombats are strong, heavy marsupials that dig long burrows, and the mother's pouch faces backwards so soil does not get in while she digs.
fact: Wombats make cube-shaped droppings, shaped by stiffer and softer parts of the gut; cubes do not roll off rocks and logs, so they work as signposts, and the scientists who found this won an Ig Nobel Prize in 2019.
fact: Tasmanian devils are the biggest meat-eating marsupials alive; they screech and growl to sort out who eats first, and their strong jaws crunch whole carcasses, bones and fur included.
fact: A rare cancer passed on through bites to the face killed most wild devils in Tasmania, so mainland zoos keep healthy «insurance» devils and have released some back to the wild.
fact: A platypus shuts its eyes, ears and nose under water and finds shrimps and worms by feeling tiny electric signals from their muscles with its bill; males have a venomous spur on the back legs.
fact: The echidna has spines like a hedgehog, curls up or digs in when touched to protect its soft belly, lays eggs, and feeds its babies milk that oozes from patches of skin, because it has no nipples.
fact: Bilbies are small desert marsupials with huge ears that rarely need to drink; foxes and rabbits brought from Europe harmed them, so Australians sell chocolate bilbies at Easter to raise money to protect them.
fact: A sugar glider has a skin flap between its front and back legs and glides from tree to tree; it looks like a flying squirrel but is a marsupial that evolved the same trick separately.
exercise: Why does a koala sleep or rest up to about 20 hours a day? | Its eucalyptus food gives little energy and is hard to digest | The eucalyptus leaves make koalas drunk, dizzy and sleepy | It hunts insects all night long and so must rest all day | Koalas are too lazy to look for food
exercise: Which animal is a koala most closely related to? | The wombat | The brown bear | The monkey | The raccoon
exercise: Why must zoos outside Australia grow or fly in eucalyptus? | Koalas eat only a few kinds of eucalyptus leaves | Koalas also need to sleep on beds of eucalyptus wood | Eucalyptus is the only plant keepers can buy | The leaves keep the koala's fur white
exercise: Why does a koala hug a tree trunk on a hot day? | The trunk is cooler than the air and takes away heat | It is scared of falling off the branch in the heat | It is drinking water from the bark | It is rubbing the trunk to mark the tree with its strong smell
exercise: What surprising thing do koalas share with people? | Fingerprints that look very like ours | Exactly the same number of teeth | The same favourite food and drink | Blood of the same colour
exercise: Why does a wombat's pouch face backwards? | So soil does not get in while the mother digs | So the baby can look back and see where it has been | So rain can run in to wash the baby | So the baby can fall out when ready
exercise: What shape are wombat droppings? | Cubes | Long spirals | Perfect balls | Flat discs
exercise: Why are cube-shaped droppings useful to a wombat? | They do not roll off rocks, so they mark its land | They are easier to carry back and stack up in the burrow | They are eaten again by the babies | They help it count how many meals it ate
exercise: Why do Tasmanian devils screech while feeding together? | To sort out who eats first without much fighting | To scare away rain clouds | Because their food is far too hot to eat quietly | To call kangaroos to join them
exercise: How did the face cancer spread among Tasmanian devils? | Through bites to the face when they fought over food | Through dirty water in the rivers where they drank | Through the air like a cold | From eating eucalyptus leaves
exercise: How does a platypus find food under water? | It feels tiny electric signals with its bill | It looks with its big eyes wide open | It listens hard with both of its ears wide open | It follows the smell with its nose
exercise: What is dangerous about a male platypus? | A venomous spur on its back legs | A poisonous bill | Rows of teeth as sharp as a shark's | Spines on its tail
exercise: How does an echidna feed its babies milk? | Milk oozes from patches of skin, as it has no nipples | It brings them ants soaked in milk from a cow | It does not feed them milk at all | From nipples like a cat's
exercise: Why are chocolate bilbies sold at Easter in Australia? | To raise money to protect bilbies | Because bilbies lay chocolate eggs | Because rabbits are banned from shops | Because bilbies are a kind of bunny
exercise: How does a sugar glider travel between trees? | It glides on a skin flap between its legs | It flies by flapping its little feathery wings | It jumps with very long back legs | It swings on its tail like a monkey
exercise: Is a sugar glider a kind of flying squirrel? | No, it is a marsupial that evolved gliding separately | Yes, it is the smallest flying squirrel | Yes, it is a kind of squirrel that just happens to have a pouch | No, it is a kind of bat
test: «koala bears are just stoned on eucalyptus lol» What is right? | Koalas are not bears and not drugged; their food gives little energy | Yes, the leaves make them dizzy and sleepy, like a strong drug | Yes, and that is why they fall from trees | No, koalas sleep because they hunt at night
test: Taz from Looney Tunes spins like a tornado and eats anything. What is true of real devils? | They do not spin, but they screech and eat bones and fur | They spin round fast like a tornado whenever they are angry | They only eat leaves and grass | They are a kind of small bear
test: A police officer finds a fingerprint on a tree in an Australian forest. Why might it be a puzzle? | Koala fingerprints look very like human ones | Kangaroos leave lots of fingerprints on trees | Trees make their own fingerprints | Wombats paint their fingerprints
test: «wombat poo's square?? someone's carving it lol» What is true? | Wombats make cube-shaped droppings in their own gut | Yes, the keepers carve it into cubes for visitors | Wombats press each dropping flat against square stones | It is not poo but soil from digging
test: 3 wombats and 2 koalas sleep in the nocturnal room. How many ears altogether? | 10 | 5 | 20 | 12
test: What would happen if a mother wombat's pouch faced forwards like a kangaroo's? | Soil would fly into it while she digs | The baby would see better and grow faster | Nothing at all would change | The baby would dig its own burrow
test: «saw a platypus at the creek, gonna grab it» What is the best answer? | Don't: males have a venomous spur that hurts a lot | Fine, platypuses are gentle and tame, and they never hurt anyone | Fine, if you hold it by the bill | Fine, it only bites under water
test: Why might scientists release healthy zoo devils back into Tasmania? | To rebuild wild numbers after the face cancer killed many | To eat all the koalas | Because zoos have run out of food and space for them | To teach the wild devils to spin
test: «echidna's just an Aussie hedgehog» What is right? | It looks like one but is an egg-laying mammal | Yes, it is the same animal with a new name | Yes, hedgehogs in Australia grow longer noses | No, it is a kind of porcupine bird
test: Why is the koala not a bear, even though cartoons call it one? | It is a marsupial whose baby grows in a pouch | It is too small to be a bear | It cannot growl or roar like a real bear does | Bears do not live in trees
test: A sugar glider and a flying squirrel both glide. What does this show? | Different animals can evolve the same trick separately | They are exactly the same animal living on two continents | The sugar glider copied the squirrel | Gliding animals are all rodents
test: 4 bilbies and 1 sugar glider in the nocturnal room. How many tails? | 5 | 4 | 10 | 1
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the prolonged rest of the koala? | It conserves the scant energy furnished by eucalyptus leaves, which are poisonous and difficult to digest | It results from intoxication by the eucalyptus leaf, which acts upon the koala as a strong drug | It is undertaken by day because the koala hunts by night | It is observed only during the winter months
exam: Identify the means by which the platypus detects its prey beneath the water. | By perceiving minute electrical signals with its bill, its eyes, ears and nostrils being closed | By sight, its eyes being fully open | By hearing, its ears remaining open | By the sense of smell alone
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the droppings of the wombat. | They are cubic in form, being shaped within the gut, and serve to mark its territory | They are spherical in form and are buried deep within the burrow by the mother | They are carved into shape by the animal's claws | They are spiral in form and are consumed by the young
exam: State the principal cause of the decline of the Tasmanian devil in the wild. | A transmissible cancer passed on by bites to the face | The destruction of all the eucalyptus forest in Tasmania | Predation by the koala | A shortage of fresh water in Tasmania
